ated and so the consequence  of that they avoid too much scrutiny but  my exposure i worked for eight years at  twitter my exposure is that if people  knew the amount of um the amount of  employees working on responding to to to  issues or if people knew the amount of  people working to keep  toxic tweets away from them if they if  they knew how small those teams were  then they would have an opinion on it  and my view is that every platform from  facebook to youtube to twitter  should have to publish the amount of  people they've got working responding to  customer inquiries responding to  if someone he's receiving abuse they  should know that the team responding to  that is only three people  i think if we knew those things and if  our politicians were able to to  uh know those things it would  fundamentally change the the dialogue  the discourse that takes place if we  knew the rules and the rules were  published then we could say okay we  don't believe those rules are strict  enough so we often see these things only  when they intrude into the news  premiership football is subjected to  racist abuse and the first thing we say  is how did this happen well if we knew  that it happened because there was a  small team uh the size of the  the uber passengers in a car in and  they're dealing with it in hungary if we  knew that all of those things were the  components they would say well now it's  obvious that that was going to happen  and i think if there was that degree  more transparency it would actually  improve our ability to to police these  things and hold them uh hold these  platforms accountable  did you make it all sound a little bit  sinister do you now on reflection  looking back as somebody who's worked  within it do you think it slightly is  no i mean i'm a big fan of how these  platforms can work but broadly you have  to understand that the business model of  silicon valley is it's very convenient  for silicon valley platforms to say look  we're going t